##DESCRIPTION
##  Algebra problem: true or false for inequality
##ENDDESCRIPTION

##KEYWORDS('algebra', 'inequality', 'fraction')

## DBsubject('Numerical Analysis')
## DBchapter('Sequences')
## DBsection('Order of Convergence')
## Date('2/3/2018')
## Author('Peter Staab')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserRadioButtons.pl');

Context('Numeric');

($order1, $order2, $order3) = random_subset(3, 1 .. 3);

@x1      = (random(0.6, 0.99, 0.01));
$const1  = random(0.1, 0.9, 0.01);
$lambda1 = Compute($const1);

@x2      = (random(0.6, 0.99, 0.01));
$const2  = random(0.1, 0.9, 0.01);
$lambda2 = Compute($const2);

@x3      = (random(0.6, 0.99, 0.01));
$const3  = random(0.1, 0.9, 0.01);
$lambda3 = Compute($const3);

for ($i = 0; $i < 8; $i++) {
    push(@x1,
        $const1 * $x1[$i]**$order1 +
            0.1*$const1 * $x1[$i]**($order1 + 1));
    push(@x2,
        $const2 * $x2[$i]**$order2 +
            0.1*$const2 * $x2[$i]**($order2 + 1));
    push(@x3,
        $const3 * $x3[$i]**$order3 +
            0.1*$const3 * $x3[$i]**($order3 + 1));
}

$radio1 = RadioButtons(
    [ 'linear', 'quadratic', 'cubic' ],
    $order1 - 1,
    separator => '&nbsp;&nbsp;'
);
$radio2 = RadioButtons(
    [ 'linear', 'quadratic', 'cubic' ],
    $order2 - 1,
    separator => '&nbsp;&nbsp;'
);
$radio3 = RadioButtons(
    [ 'linear', 'quadratic', 'cubic' ],
    $order3 - 1,
    separator => '&nbsp;&nbsp;'
);

BEGIN_PGML
Below is a table of errors for 3 different methods (each column).  Determine numerically the order of convergence (linear, quadratic, cubic) and estimate the asymptotic error constant in each case.

[#
    [.A.][.B.][.C.]*{headerrow => 1}
    [. [$x1[0]] .] [. [$x2[0]] .] [. [$x3[0]] .]*
    [. [$x1[1]] .] [. [$x2[1]] .] [. [$x3[1]] .]*
    [. [$x1[2]] .] [. [$x2[2]] .] [. [$x3[2]] .]*
    [. [$x1[3]] .] [. [$x2[3]] .] [. [$x3[3]] .]*
    [. [$x1[4]] .] [. [$x2[4]] .] [. [$x3[4]] .]*
    [. [$x1[5]] .] [. [$x2[5]] .] [. [$x3[5]] .]*
    [. [$x1[6]] .] [. [$x2[6]] .] [. [$x3[6]] .]*
#]

*Method A:*

Order: [__]{$radio1}

Asymptotic Error Constant: [_____]{Real($const1)->with(tolerance=>.1)->cmp()}

*Method B:*

Order: [__]{$radio2}

Asymptotic Error Constant: [_____]{Real($const2)->with(tolerance=>.1)->cmp()}

*Method C:*

Order: [__]{$radio3}

Asymptotic Error Constant: [_____]{Real($const3)->with(tolerance=>.1)->cmp()}



END_PGML

############################
#  Solution

# the variables errAn is the error sequence of A given for order n
@errRatA = ();
@errRatB = ();
@errRatC = ();

for ($i = 0; $i < 5; $i++) {
    push(@errRatA,
        log($x1[ $i + 2 ] / $x1[ $i + 1 ]) / log($x1[ $i + 1 ] / $x1[$i]));
    push(@errRatB,
        log($x2[ $i + 2 ] / $x2[ $i + 1 ]) / log($x2[ $i + 1 ] / $x2[$i]));
    push(@errRatC,
        log($x3[ $i + 2 ] / $x3[ $i + 1 ]) / log($x3[ $i + 1 ] / $x3[$i]));
}

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Recall that in the limit 

[``` \alpha = \frac{\ln(\epsilon_{n+2}/\epsilon_{n+1})}{\ln(\epsilon_{n+1}/\epsilon_{n})} ```]

and then for this value of [`\alpha`], the asymptotic error constant is 

[``` \lambda = \frac{\epsilon_{n+1}}{\epsilon_n^\alpha} ```]

So we compute both of these for each sequence. 

*Error Sequence A*

[#
    [.[`n`] .] [.[``\frac{\ln(\epsilon_{n+2}/\epsilon_{n+1})}{\ln(\epsilon_{n+1}/\epsilon_{n})})``].]*{headerrow => 1}
    [. [`0`] .] [.[$errRatA[0].]*
    [. [`1`] .] [.[$errRatA[1].]*
    [. [`2`] .] [.[$errRatA[2].]*
    [. [`3`] .] [.[$errRatA[3].]*
    [. [`4`] .] [.[$errRatA[4].]*
#]

And as can be scene from the [`\alpha = [$order1]`] column, the ratio is constant, so the order of method [`A`] is [$order1] and the asymptotic error constant is
[``` \lambda \approx \frac{\epsilon_{n+2}}{\epsilon_{n+1}^{\alpha}} 
= \frac{[$x1[5]]}{([$x1[4]])^{[$order1]}} = [@ $x1[5]/$x1[4]**($order1) @]
```]

*Error Sequence B*

[#
    [.[`n`] .] [.[``\frac{\ln(\epsilon_{n+2}/\epsilon_{n+1})}{\ln(\epsilon_{n+1}/\epsilon_{n})})``].]*{headerrow => 1}
    [. [`0`] .] [.[$errRatB[0].]*
    [. [`1`] .] [.[$errRatB[1].]*
    [. [`2`] .] [.[$errRatB[2].]*
    [. [`3`] .] [.[$errRatB[3].]*
    [. [`4`] .] [.[$errRatB[4].]*
#]


And as can be scene from the [`\alpha = [$order2]`] column, the ratio is constant, so the order of method [`B`] is [$order2] and the asymptotic error constant is 
[``` \lambda \approx \frac{\epsilon_{n+2}}{\epsilon_{n+1}^{\alpha}} 
= \frac{[$x2[5]]}{([$x2[4]])^{[$order2]}} = [@ $x2[5]/$x2[4]**($order2) @]
```]

*Error Sequence C*

[#
    [.[`n`] .] [.[``\frac{\ln(\epsilon_{n+2}/\epsilon_{n+1})}{\ln(\epsilon_{n+1}/\epsilon_{n})})``].]*{headerrow => 1}
    [. [`0`] .] [.[$errRatC[0].]*
    [. [`1`] .] [.[$errRatC[1].]*
    [. [`2`] .] [.[$errRatC[2].]*
    [. [`3`] .] [.[$errRatC[3].]*
    [. [`4`] .] [.[$errRatC[4].]*
#]


And as can be scene from the [`\alpha = [$order3]`] column, the ratio is constant, so the order of method [`C`] is [$order3] and the asymptotic error constant is 
[``` \lambda \approx \frac{\epsilon_{n+2}}{\epsilon_{n+1}^{\alpha}} 
= \frac{[$x3[5]]}{([$x3[4]])^{[$order3]}} = [@ $x3[5]/$x3[4]**($order3) @]
```]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
